A["stage3/B1/g2/o6/substitution"] = J({
 "Title": "Harmonic-mean p-value fusion with region-mass scoring: per-image MVFA scores that stay valid under dependent levels",
 "Summary": "Replace Fisher's combination, which assumes independent evidence, with the harmonic mean p-value across MVFA's strongly correlated levels and branches, keeping region-mass pooling and support-derived thresholds.",
 "Hypothesis": "Harmonic-mean p-value fusion gives realised false-positive rates at the support threshold within 3 points of nominal, versus more than 8 points of excess false positives for Fisher fusion, on held-out BMAD modalities with K >= 4, with image AUROC within 0.5 points of Fisher fusion.",
 "Rationale": "The four MVFA levels are sequential stages of one ViT and the two branches share the backbone, so their per-patch p-values are strongly positively dependent; Fisher's statistic assumes independence and is anti-conservative under positive dependence, which inflates false positives exactly where calibration is the goal. The harmonic mean p-value is a combination rule that remains approximately valid under arbitrary dependence, so substituting it keeps the parent's framing (support-anchored per-pixel evidence, region pooling, support thresholds) while fixing a statistical flaw the parent inherits.",
 "Method": "1) Per-pixel empirical p-values p_bl as in Fisher-MVFA (support LOO nulls). 2) HMP per pixel: H = 8 / sum_{b,l} (1/p_bl); statistic X = -log H. 3) Region-mass pooling on X with t_pix = 99th percentile of support LOO X; image score as before. 4) Report the empirical correlation matrix of the 8 per-pixel p-values on support normals to document dependence. 5) Code: scoring.py hmp_map(); reuse region_mass().",
 "Experiment": PROT_B + " Baselines: Fisher + region mass (parent); Bonferroni-style min-p; LOO-Z + region mass. Metrics: realised FPR at nominal 5% and 1%, sensitivity, image/pixel AUROC, PRO, p-value correlation across levels. Falsification: no FPR improvement over Fisher fusion.",
 "Provenance": [MADCLIP, RECAP, AA, PSMP]})
A["stage3/A1/g2/o4/substitution"] = J({
 "Title": "Position-aware normal-bank pseudo-masks: matching each patch only to normal patches from the same anatomical neighbourhood",
 "Summary": "Keep normal-bank pseudo-masks from image labels but substitute global nearest-neighbour search with a neighbourhood-restricted search over normal patches at the same grid position (+/- 2 patches), so anatomy that is normal elsewhere cannot hide an anomaly.",
 "Hypothesis": "Position-aware matching raises pseudo-mask IoU by at least 0.08 over global kNN on BrainMRI and LiverCT training images (roughly spatially aligned) without reducing it on RESC, and the resulting segmentation adapters gain at least 2 held-out PRO points over global-kNN pseudo-masks.",
 "Rationale": "The parent's reviewed weakness is that global nearest-neighbour distances may fail for subtle medical anomalies: a lesion patch can resemble normal tissue from a different location (e.g. a hyperintense lesion resembling normal ventricles or skull), giving a small distance and a missed pseudo-mask. Brain MRI slices in BMAD come from co-registered volumes and liver CT slices are roughly centred, so restricting matches to the same spatial neighbourhood is a better normality model. The substitution keeps the parent's framing (image labels define normality, pseudo-masks supervise the segmentation branch) and its evaluation.",
 "Method": "1) For each source dataset, store normal patch tokens per grid position (17x17) and level, coreset 20% per position. 2) For a patch at (u, v) of an abnormal image, distance = 1 - max cosine over bank tokens at positions within +/-2 in u and v. 3) Pseudo-mask = patches above the 99th percentile of the same score on held-out normal images of that dataset, foreground-restricted. 4) Datasets without spatial alignment (OCT17, ChestXray, HIS): a neighbourhood radius selected per dataset on its normal images (largest radius at which normal-vs-normal false positives stay below 1%). 5) Train segmentation adapters with Focal + Dice; code: make_bank_masks.py with position index.",
 "Experiment": PROT_A + " Baselines: global kNN pseudo-masks (parent); mask-supervised MVFA; asymmetric MIL. Ablations: radius 0/1/2/4; coreset ratio. Metrics: pseudo-mask IoU per dataset (analysis), held-out pixel AUROC, PRO, image AUROC. Falsification: no IoU gain on BrainMRI and LiverCT.",
 "Provenance": [MLCA, AA, PSMP]})
A["stage3/B1/g2/o2/out_of_box"] = J({
 "Title": "Neyman-Pearson likelihood-ratio scoring: a support-normal density against a cross-modality abnormal density learned on source data",
 "Summary": "Score each test patch by the log-ratio between a density of 'generic abnormal' patch features learned from source modalities' abnormal images and a density of the target's support-normal patches, giving the most powerful per-image test at a chosen false-positive level.",
 "Hypothesis": "Likelihood-ratio scores combining a target support-normal Gaussian and a source-abnormal Gaussian mixture improve image AUROC by at least 1.5 points over normal-only Fisher fusion on held-out BMAD modalities at K = 2 and K = 4, while the support-derived threshold keeps realised false positives within 5 points of nominal.",
 "Rationale": "Fisher-MVFA asks only 'how unusual is this patch for a normal image of this modality?', the classical one-class question. By the Neyman-Pearson lemma, the most powerful test at a fixed false-positive rate compares the likelihood under normal and under abnormal hypotheses. MVFA's adapted features are trained to make abnormal patches of all modalities move in a common 'abnormal' direction (alignment to the abnormal text anchor), so an abnormal density estimated on source modalities may transfer, while the normal density must come from the target supports. This turns the scoring problem into a two-sided test without target anomalies.",
 "Method": "1) Abnormal model: on source modalities' abnormal images, collect adapted patch tokens from the top-2% patches (images with masks: true lesion patches, used only for source-side density fitting; images without masks: top-2% by zero-shot score), PCA to 64 dims (fitted on source), Gaussian mixture with 8 components. 2) Normal model: Ledoit-Wolf Gaussian on the target supports' patch tokens in the same PCA space. 3) Patch score = log p_abn(f) - log p_nor(f), summed over levels; image score = top-2% mean. 4) Threshold: 95th percentile of support LOO image scores. 5) Code: fit_abnormal_gmm.py, scoring.py lr_score(); no adapter retraining.",
 "Experiment": PROT_B + " Baselines: Fisher fusion; Mahalanobis normal-only; transductive MVFA. Ablations: number of components; abnormal model from images without masks only; PCA dimension. Metrics: image/pixel AUROC, realised FPR, sensitivity at the threshold. Falsification: AUROC gain below 0.5 points.",
 "Provenance": [MAD, AA]})
A["stage3/B1/g2/o5/out_of_box"] = J({
 "Title": "Adaptive S-norm for MVFA: cohort score normalisation borrowed from speaker verification",
 "Summary": "Normalise each test image's anomaly score both against the target supports (Z-norm) and against how the same image scores against memory banks built from a cohort of other modalities' normal images (T-norm), and average the two, as speaker-verification systems do to make one threshold work across speakers.",
 "Hypothesis": "Adaptive S-norm reduces the spread of the optimal threshold across held-out BMAD modalities by at least 40% and improves balanced accuracy at a single global threshold by at least 3 points over support-only normalisation, with image AUROC within 1 point.",
 "Rationale": "Speaker verification faced the same problem as per-image MVFA scoring: raw match scores are not comparable across speakers, so one threshold fails. Its solution is cohort normalisation: Z-norm uses the enrolment model's score distribution on impostor data, T-norm uses the test utterance's scores against a cohort of other models, and S-norm averages them, giving thresholds that transfer across speakers. In MVFA, the 'enrolment' is the target support bank and a natural cohort is the normal images of the source modalities: if a test image is unusual even against unrelated normal banks only to the same degree as normal target images are, it is not anomalous.",
 "Method": "1) Cohort: for each source modality, a memory bank from 16 normal validation images (adapted tokens per level). 2) For a test image x: raw few-shot image score s(x) against the target support bank; Z-norm: (s - mu_Z)/sigma_Z with statistics of support LOO scores; T-norm: (s - mu_T(x))/sigma_T(x) where mu_T, sigma_T are the mean/std of x's scores against the top-10 most similar cohort banks (adaptive cohort). 3) S-norm = (Z + T)/2, fused with the zero-shot branch's Z-norm score; single global threshold selected on source episodes. 4) Code: build_cohort.py; scoring.py snorm(); no retraining.",
 "Experiment": PROT_B + " Baselines: LOO-Z (Z-norm only); Fisher fusion; transductive MVFA. Metrics: AUROC, balanced accuracy and FPR at one global threshold across all held-out modalities, spread of per-modality optimal thresholds. Falsification: no reduction in threshold spread.",
 "Provenance": [FED, MLCA]})
A["stage3/B1/g2/o4/out_of_box"] = J({
 "Title": "Risk-controlled abstention band: per-image MVFA decisions that defer when support evidence is insufficient",
 "Summary": "Turn per-image MVFA scores into three-way decisions (normal, abnormal, defer) using a lower and an upper threshold calibrated on source-modality episodes to control the error rate on non-deferred images, reporting risk-coverage curves instead of a single AUROC.",
 "Hypothesis": "With thresholds calibrated on source episodes for a 5% error target, the abstention band keeps the error rate on non-deferred target images at or below 7% while deferring at most 30% of images for K >= 4, whereas a single threshold at the same coverage has an error rate above 12% on at least two held-out modalities.",
 "Rationale": "The parent learns one calibration mapping from source episodes and must commit on every image, which the reviews noted is fragile when the source-to-target transfer of calibration fails. By analogy with selective prediction in safety-critical systems (and with how radiologists escalate uncertain studies), a detector can be made reliable by abstaining in the score region where source episodes show that errors concentrate. The deferral rate itself becomes a measurable, clinically meaningful cost, directly addressing the uncertainty gap in MVFA's evaluation.",
 "Method": "1) Per-image score: LOO-Z or Fisher fused score (support-anchored). 2) On source episodes (200 per source modality), choose (t_low, t_high) minimising deferral subject to error on non-deferred images <= 5% (Learn-then-Test style procedure with a binomial bound at confidence 0.9). 3) Target: normal if score < t_low, abnormal if > t_high, defer otherwise. 4) Report risk-coverage curves and the area under them. 5) Code: calibrate_abstention.py; scoring.py decide(); no retraining.",
 "Experiment": PROT_B + " Baselines: single support threshold; episode-calibrated single threshold. Metrics: error on non-deferred images, deferral rate, risk-coverage AUC, sensitivity on non-deferred abnormal images. Falsification: error target violated by more than 3 points.",
 "Provenance": [FED]})
A["stage3/A1/g2/o2/out_of_box"] = J({
 "Title": "Change detection against a retrieved normal twin: optimal-transport pseudo-masks from image labels",
 "Summary": "For each abnormal source image, retrieve its most similar normal image from the same dataset, align their patch tokens with entropic optimal transport, and treat the patch mass of the abnormal image that cannot be transported cheaply as the pseudo-mask for MVFA's segmentation adapters.",
 "Hypothesis": "Unbalanced-OT residual mass against a retrieved normal twin yields pseudo-masks with at least 0.05 higher IoU than counterfactual-replacement masks on BrainMRI, LiverCT and RESC training images, and segmentation adapters trained on them retain at least 80% of mask-supervised MVFA's held-out pixel-AUROC margin over chance.",
 "Rationale": "By analogy with change detection in remote sensing, where a before/after pair reveals what changed, an abnormal image paired with an anatomically similar normal image reveals the lesion as the part that has no counterpart. Image labels provide the normal pool; global CLIP features retrieve the twin; unbalanced optimal transport handles small misalignments and lets lesion patches remain unmatched instead of being forced onto normal tissue. Unlike counterfactual replacement, this does not depend on the detection branch's mean-pooled score being sensitive to single patches.",
 "Method": "1) Twin retrieval: frozen CLIP CLS cosine within the same source dataset's normal training images (top-1, or average over top-3). 2) Patch tokens at levels 2-3 (mid-level, more spatial); cost C_ij = 1 - cos; unbalanced Sinkhorn (POT library, epsilon 0.05, marginal relaxation 0.5). 3) Residual mass per abnormal patch = 1 - transported mass; pseudo-mask = patches above the 95th percentile of residual on normal-normal pairs from the same dataset. 4) Train segmentation adapters with Focal + Dice on pseudo-masks. 5) Code: make_ot_masks.py; train_zero.py.",
 "Experiment": PROT_A + " Baselines: counterfactual replacement; normal-bank kNN; mask-supervised MVFA. Ablations: number of twins; epsilon and relaxation; levels used. Metrics: pseudo-mask IoU (analysis), held-out pixel AUROC, PRO, image AUROC, preprocessing time. Falsification: IoU not above counterfactual masks.",
 "Provenance": [MADCLIP, RECAP]})
A["stage3/A1/g2/o5/out_of_box"] = J({
 "Title": "Adversarial erasing for full lesion extent: iterative MIL that must still find the anomaly after its most obvious part is erased",
 "Summary": "Train MVFA's segmentation adapters with image-level MIL in rounds; after each round, erase (replace by normal-bank tokens) the top-scoring region of each abnormal training image and require the remaining patches to still carry anomaly evidence, so localisation grows from the most discriminative patches to the whole lesion.",
 "Hypothesis": "Two erasing rounds increase held-out PRO by at least 3 points and pixel AUROC by at least 1 point over single-round top-k MIL, while image AUROC changes by less than 1 point.",
 "Rationale": "Counterfactual replacement and MIL both find the patches that matter most for the decision, which is a known failure mode of weakly supervised localisation: only the most discriminative part of an object is found. Adversarial erasing in weakly supervised semantic segmentation solves this by erasing the discovered region and forcing the model to find additional evidence. Medical lesions (tumours, retinal fluid) are spatially extended, so recovering full extent matters directly for PRO and pixel AUROC.",
 "Method": "1) Round 1: top-k MIL (k = 6) with the all-patches-normal term for normal images. 2) Erase: for each abnormal training image, replace adapted tokens of its top-k patches by nearest normal-bank tokens (10% coreset of the dataset's normal images) at all levels. 3) Round 2: continue training with MIL on erased abnormal images labelled abnormal only if their erased image score from round 1 remains above the dataset's normal 95th percentile (otherwise the image is excluded from the positive set, preventing forced false evidence). 4) Union of discovered regions across rounds forms soft pseudo-masks for a final Focal + Dice stage (optional ablation). 5) Code: train_zero.py erasing loop; loss.py topk_mil.",
 "Experiment": PROT_A + " Baselines: single-round MIL; asymmetric SigLIP-MIL; mask-supervised MVFA. Ablations: number of rounds (1-3); erasing by zeroing vs normal-bank replacement; final pseudo-mask stage on/off. Metrics: held-out pixel AUROC, PRO, image AUROC; recovered lesion area ratio (analysis). Falsification: PRO gain below 1 point.",
 "Provenance": [MADCLIP, RECAP]})
A["stage3/A1/g2/o6/out_of_box"] = J({
 "Title": "Patch-level positive-unlabelled learning: normal-image patches as labelled negatives, abnormal-image patches as unlabelled",
 "Summary": "Recast mask-free training of MVFA's segmentation adapters as positive-unlabelled (PU) learning at the patch level, using the non-negative PU risk estimator with a per-dataset class prior (expected lesion fraction) estimated from image-labelled data.",
 "Hypothesis": "Non-negative PU learning with an estimated patch-level class prior retains at least 85% of mask-supervised MVFA's held-out pixel-AUROC margin over chance and outperforms asymmetric top-k MIL by at least 1.5 pixel-AUROC points, because it uses every patch of abnormal images rather than only the top-k.",
 "Rationale": "The parent's key insight — every patch of a normal image is normal — is exactly the labelled side of a PU problem: here the known class is 'normal' (labelled negatives) and abnormal images contribute an unlabelled mixture of normal and abnormal patches. PU learning theory gives an unbiased risk estimator for this setting once the mixture proportion (the fraction of anomalous patches in abnormal images) is known; MIL's top-k is a heuristic stand-in for that proportion. The proportion can be estimated without masks by mixture proportion estimation between normal-image and abnormal-image patch score distributions.",
 "Method": "1) Patch classifier = MVFA's segmentation-branch patch-text logit z = t(cos(f, T_abn) - cos(f, T_nor)) + b. 2) Class prior pi_d for each source dataset d: mixture proportion estimation (best-bin estimator) on the zero-shot patch scores of normal vs abnormal images. 3) nnPU loss: with N = normal-image patches (labelled 'normal') and U = abnormal-image patches with mixing weight pi, R = (1 - pi) E_N[l(z, normal)]... implemented with the standard nnPU estimator (Kiryo et al.) where the negative class is labelled, max(0, ...) correction on the unlabelled term. 4) Sigmoid loss, lr 1e-4, 50 epochs; balanced batches of 4 normal + 4 abnormal images from the same dataset. 5) Code: loss.py nnpu_loss(); estimate_prior.py; train_zero.py.",
 "Experiment": PROT_A + " Baselines: asymmetric SigLIP-MIL; top-k MIL; mask-supervised MVFA. Ablations: prior misspecification (x0.5, x2); estimated vs oracle prior (from masks, analysis only). Metrics: held-out pixel AUROC, PRO, image AUROC; prior estimation error (analysis). Falsification: no gain over asymmetric MIL.",
 "Provenance": [MADCLIP, RECAP]})
A["stage3/A1/g2/o1/out_of_box"] = J({
 "Title": "Context-surprisal pseudo-masks: a masked feature predictor trained on normal images flags patches it cannot predict",
 "Summary": "Train a small transformer to predict masked patch tokens from their context on normal source images only; on abnormal images, patches whose tokens are poorly predicted from surrounding tissue form pseudo-masks for MVFA's segmentation adapters.",
 "Hypothesis": "Context-surprisal pseudo-masks reach at least the IoU of global normal-bank kNN masks on BrainMRI, LiverCT and RESC training images while producing at least 30% fewer pseudo-positive patches on normal images, and segmentation adapters trained on them retain at least 80% of mask-supervised MVFA's held-out pixel-AUROC margin over chance.",
 "Rationale": "By analogy with surprisal in masked language models, a token is anomalous when its context makes it unlikely. Normal-bank kNN asks whether a patch resembles any normal patch anywhere, which ignores context (a patch can be normal tissue in the wrong place); a masked predictor trained on normal images learns which content is expected at a location given its surroundings, so lesions in otherwise normal anatomy are surprising even when their appearance exists elsewhere. Image labels are again enough: only normal images are used for training the predictor.",
 "Method": "1) Masked feature predictor: 4-layer transformer (width 256) on frozen CLIP level-3 patch tokens (17x17) of normal source images; mask 30% of patches in random blocks; predict masked tokens with a cosine loss; 30 epochs. 2) Surprisal of patch j in an abnormal image = mean over 8 random masks covering j of (1 - cos(pred_j, token_j)). 3) Pseudo-mask = patches above the 99th percentile of surprisal on held-out normal images of the same dataset. 4) Train segmentation adapters with Focal + Dice on pseudo-masks. 5) Code: train_mfp.py (about 2M parameters, fits easily on one GPU), make_surprisal_masks.py, train_zero.py.",
 "Experiment": PROT_A + " Baselines: normal-bank kNN pseudo-masks; mask-supervised MVFA; asymmetric MIL. Ablations: predictor depth; level of tokens; mask ratio. Metrics: pseudo-mask IoU and normal-image false-positive rate (analysis), held-out pixel AUROC, PRO, image AUROC. Falsification: IoU below kNN masks and no FP reduction.",
 "Provenance": [MLCA, AA, PSMP]})
